  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Vertically stacked low pressure approx 360 mi\neast of the NC/VA border will continue to slowly drift northward\ntoday, then will quickly accelerate northeastward Thursday as a\nnorthern stream shortwave digs into the Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast. Winds will continue to gradually east today as the\nlow drifts farther away from the area but still could see gusts\naround 30-40 mph along the Outer Banks today. \n\nThe strong winds offshore continue to bring large surf along \nthe coast as well as coastal flooding impacts along vulnerable \nlocations of the coast, especially along Hatteras and Ocracoke \nIslands where dune structure is fragile. The National Parks \nService and NCDOT have reported substantial beach erosion from \nsouth Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant overwash \noccurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road \nconditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the Marc \nBasnight Bridge to Rodanthe, and ferry service remains suspended\nin certain locations. Additionally, webcams last evening show \nat least one section of the Pea Island dune structure has been \nwashed away. Each subsequent high tide cycle will continue to \nthreaten the dunes further, and the threat of additional impacts\nto Hwy 12 remain. Tides are expected \n\nMinor soundside flooding is also a concern along southern \nportions of the Pamlico Sound, including Frisco and Ocracoke. A \nCoastal Flood Warning remains in effect for Hatteras and \nOcracoke Islands and a Coastal Flood Advisory remains in \neffect for the Northern Outer Banks. \n\nAdditionally, a high surf advisory continues for Cape Hatteras \nand points north through the OBX where surf height up to 8-12\nft will continue. Have cancelled the High Surf Advisory south of\nHatteras as seas have subsided below 8 ft. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us\nthrough the next several days with temps a bit below climo. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s range. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the 40s inland to the 50s coast.",
